    {
      "parameters": {
        "content": "## Score WhatsApp leads with AI and send reminders and onboarding over Twilio\n\n**Who's it for**\nSmall service businesses running customer conversations over WhatsApp who want three jobs handled\nwithout staff: qualifying an inbound enquiry, chasing an overdue invoice, and welcoming a new\ncustomer.\n\n**How it works**\nThree independent flows share one Twilio WhatsApp number.\n\n**Lead capture** — an inbound WhatsApp message goes to an AI agent that returns a structured lead\nscore against a fixed schema, so the reply is a typed object rather than free text. The score is\nstored and an answer is sent straight back.\n\n**Collections** — on a schedule, overdue invoices are read, the customer record for each is looked\nup, and a payment reminder goes out on WhatsApp.\n\n**Onboarding** — a submitted form creates the customer record and triggers a welcome message.\n\n**How to set up**\n1. Attach your Twilio credentials to the three send nodes and set your WhatsApp sender number.\n2. Attach an OpenRouter credential to the model node and pick a model.\n3. Create the data tables for invoices, customers and lead scores.\n4. Send a WhatsApp message to your Twilio number to test the lead flow end to end.\n\n**Requirements**\nA Twilio account with WhatsApp enabled, an OpenRouter API key, and n8n data tables for customers,\ninvoices and lead scores.\n\n**How to customize**\nThe scoring rubric is the prompt on **`score the lead with AI`**, and the shape it must return is\n**`lead score schema`** — change them together. Each of the three flows can be deleted without\naffecting the other two.",
        "height": 1082,
        "width": 640,
        "color": 4
      },
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
      "typeVersion": 1,
      "position": [
        0,
        -1482
      ],
      "id": "sticky-00",
      "name": "Template overview"
    },
    {
      "parameters": {
        "content": "### 1. Lead capture\n\nAn inbound WhatsApp message reaches **`score the lead with AI`**, which is pinned to a schema by **`lead score schema`** hanging beneath it.\n\nThat pairing is the point: the agent must return a typed object with the fields you asked for, so **`store the lead score`** is writing structured data rather than a paragraph somebody has to parse later.\n\nChange the rubric in the agent's prompt and the schema together — they only work as a pair.",
        "height": 268,
        "width": 1040,
        "color": 5
      },
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
      "typeVersion": 1,
      "position": [
        0,
        -308
      ],
      "id": "sticky-01",
      "name": "1. Lead capture"
    },
    {
      "parameters": {
        "content": "### 2. Collections - overdue invoice reminders\n\nRuns on a schedule, and **`Run it now`** does the same pass by hand.\n\n**`read the overdue invoices`** finds what is due, then **`read the customer`** resolves who to contact before anything is sent — so a reminder can never go to the wrong number because an invoice row was missing its contact.",
        "height": 224,
        "width": 760,
        "color": 3
      },
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
      "typeVersion": 1,
      "position": [
        280,
        860
      ],
      "id": "sticky-02",
      "name": "2. Collections"
    },
    {
      "parameters": {
        "content": "### 3. Onboarding\n\nA submitted form creates the customer record first, then sends the welcome message.\n\nThat order matters: the welcome is sent only if the record was actually created, so nobody is greeted as a customer who does not exist in your tables.",
        "height": 224,
        "width": 760,
        "color": 6
      },
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
      "typeVersion": 1,
      "position": [
        0,
        1160
      ],
      "id": "sticky-03",
      "name": "3. Onboarding"
    }
  ],
